WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.960
- The only thing scarier

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.480
than being haunted by a
ghost is getting sued.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.740
And you may not believe in ghosts,

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.271
but that doesn't mean that
they don't show up in lawsuits.

00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:08.354
(people screaming)

00:00:08.354 --> 00:00:09.540
(ghosts laughing)

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.650
- Oh my God!

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:11.940
The Scoleri Brothers!

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:12.900
- Because telling ghost stories

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.875
isn't just something that
you do around a campfire

00:00:14.875 --> 00:00:17.460
or as an overused plot
device in horror movies.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.216
- Why, once I met this
guy who knew this guy

00:00:19.216 --> 00:00:20.588
who knew this guy who knew this guy

00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:21.810
who knew this guy who knew this guy..

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.988
- In fact, ghosts have actually
repeatedly been involved

00:00:23.988 --> 00:00:25.380
in litigation.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.377
- We would have to perform a sue-ance.

00:00:28.377 --> 00:00:29.210
(lightning strikes)

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.091
(spooky ambient music)

00:00:31.091 --> 00:00:32.430
- And when it comes to
the paranormal in court,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.470
judges don't simply say ghosts aren't real

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.446
and get that (bleep) outta my courtroom.

00:00:35.446 --> 00:00:37.620
Judges have repeatedly treated spirits

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.880
with a veneer of seriousness

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.650
in both civil and criminal cases.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.241
And on occasion, poltergeists
have even helped establish

00:00:43.241 --> 00:00:45.632
legal precedent that we
still teach law students

00:00:45.632 --> 00:00:46.950
to this day.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.230
So let's start with the ghost
who testified from the dead

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.700
to convict her own murderer.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.340
In July 1897, Edward Trout Shoe

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.990
was convicted of first degree murder

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.819
by a Greenbrier County West Virginia jury

00:00:56.819 --> 00:00:59.550
in the strangling death
of his wife, Zona Shoe.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.470
What makes this gruesome murder so notable

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.224
is the evidence that
sealed Mr. Shoe's fate

00:01:03.224 --> 00:01:05.393
came from a most surprising source,

00:01:05.393 --> 00:01:07.830
the ghost of his murdered wife.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.848
This was the first and
to date only instance

00:01:09.848 --> 00:01:12.382
in the US history that
testimony of a ghost

00:01:12.382 --> 00:01:15.162
has been admitted as evidence
to obtain a conviction.

00:01:15.162 --> 00:01:18.070
So how did this ghost help
convict her own killer?

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.770
Well, it's quite the story.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.628
On the morning of January 23rd, 1897

00:01:21.628 --> 00:01:23.499
the 23-yeah- old Zona Shoe was discovered

00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:26.011
by an errand boy sent to
the house by Trout Shoe.

00:01:26.011 --> 00:01:27.714
The boy found her at
the foot of the stairs,

00:01:27.714 --> 00:01:30.318
face down, dead, her
head cocked to one side.

00:01:30.318 --> 00:01:31.577
By the time the coroner arrived,

00:01:31.577 --> 00:01:34.260
Trout had moved his
wife's body to their bed

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.053
where he had dressed her in
a scarf and high necked gown.

00:01:37.053 --> 00:01:38.496
Trout was sobbing uncontrollably

00:01:38.496 --> 00:01:40.197
and showed signs of distress

00:01:40.197 --> 00:01:42.990
whenever the corner attempted
to examine the body.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.610
Now, out of respect for
the grieving spouse,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.313
the coroner stopped
his cursory examination

00:01:46.313 --> 00:01:48.770
and then wrote in his
report that Zona died

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.909
of quote "everlasting
faint," or heart attack.

00:01:51.909 --> 00:01:54.360
At Zona's wake the next
day, attendants noticed

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.832
more odd behavior from Trout.

00:01:55.832 --> 00:01:57.960
When the casket was opened for viewing,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.957
Trout immediately covered
Zona's neck with a scarf

00:01:59.957 --> 00:02:02.177
and propped her head with
a pillow and blanket.

00:02:02.177 --> 00:02:05.343
Trout also put on a display
of inconsolable grief,

00:02:05.343 --> 00:02:06.978
blocking mourners from
getting close enough

00:02:06.978 --> 00:02:08.340
to look at the body.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.007
Then one month later while Zona's mother

00:02:10.007 --> 00:02:11.389
lay wide awake at night,

00:02:11.389 --> 00:02:13.590
Zona's spirit appeared before her mother

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.290
to reveal a shocking truth
that Zona was murdered

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.880
and Trout was her killer.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.650
Zona's mother then went
to the local prosecutor

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.139
to reveal what she had learned
from her daughter's ghost.

00:02:22.139 --> 00:02:23.790
Over a period of four nights,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.320
Zona's ghost purportedly appeared

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.300
and gave a chilling account of her murder.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.772
Zona's spirit revealed
that Trout had a history

00:02:28.772 --> 00:02:30.360
of physically abusing her.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.305
When Trout became enraged

00:02:31.305 --> 00:02:33.720
one night that Zona didn't
serve meat for dinner,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.750
he grabbed his wife by the
throat, crushed her windpipe,

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.580
and snapped her neck between
the first and second vertebrae.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.320
After hearing this, the
prosecutor went to the coroner

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.634
to ask what he had
observed about Zona's neck.

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:44.002
When the coroner revealed the husband

00:02:44.002 --> 00:02:46.149
had prevented him from examining the neck,

00:02:46.149 --> 00:02:50.040
the body was exhumed and a
second autopsy was performed.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.383
They found bruises on Zona's
neck in the shape of fingers,

00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:54.660
a crushed windpipe, and her neck broken

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.280
between the first and second vertebrae,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.402
matching the mother's account
from the daughter's ghost.

00:02:59.402 --> 00:03:01.140
And in the course of investigating,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.870
the prosecutor discovered that
Shoe was not who he seemed.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.166
Edward Trout Shoe was
actually Erasmus Shoe,

00:03:06.166 --> 00:03:08.220
a blacksmith from a neighboring county.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.569
Shoe also had a history of violence

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:11.346
and was married twice before.

00:03:11.346 --> 00:03:14.417
His first wife got a divorce
after enduring physical abuse,

00:03:14.417 --> 00:03:16.619
while his second wife died
under mysterious circumstances

00:03:16.619 --> 00:03:18.630
after eight months of marriage.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.220
But after Trout was charged with a murder,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.355
there were real concerns
about securing the conviction.

00:03:22.355 --> 00:03:25.093
While Zona's neck was broken
and there were hand marks,

00:03:25.093 --> 00:03:27.840
there wasn't much hard evidence
linking Trout to the crime.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.087
The prosecutor was reluctant
to subpoena Zona's mother,

00:03:30.087 --> 00:03:31.530
fearing her ghost story

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.000
would actually damage the case,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.470
but instead of discrediting the witness,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.637
it appears that it actually bolstered

00:03:35.637 --> 00:03:36.970
the prosecution's case.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.667
Here's an excerpt from
the remarkable exchange.

00:03:39.667 --> 00:03:41.168
"Mrs. Heaster, are you positively sure

00:03:41.168 --> 00:03:43.957
that these visits are not for dreams?"

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:44.790
"Yes, sir.

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:45.623
It was not a dream.

00:03:45.623 --> 00:03:47.126
I don't dream when I'm
wide awake, to be sure,

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.124
and I know I saw her right there with me."

00:03:49.124 --> 00:03:51.026
"Are you not considerably superstitious?"

00:03:51.026 --> 00:03:52.381
"No, sir, I'm not.

00:03:52.381 --> 00:03:55.481
I was never that way
before and am not now."

00:03:55.481 --> 00:03:56.899
"Now, I would like to say if I could,

00:03:56.899 --> 00:03:59.059
to get you to say that
these were four dreams

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:00.836
and not four visions or appearances

00:04:00.836 --> 00:04:02.537
of your daughter in flesh and blood."

00:04:02.537 --> 00:04:05.820
"I'm not going to say that
for I'm not going to lie."

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.569
So pro tip, for all you aspiring
trial lawyers out there,

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:09.808
don't tell the witness
you're cross examining

00:04:09.808 --> 00:04:12.060
what you want them to admit,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.619
because they're probably
not going to do that.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.750
Ultimately, the jury convicted Trout

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.610
with just over an hour of deliberation.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.990
He was sentenced to life, which ended up

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.330
only being three years
when he died in prison.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:22.337
But that brings us to the medium

00:04:22.337 --> 00:04:24.387
who faced an extra large copyright lawsuit

00:04:24.387 --> 00:04:27.551
after claiming that Mark
Twain's ghost wrote her book.

00:04:27.551 --> 00:04:29.178
Because at the turn of the 20th century,

00:04:29.178 --> 00:04:31.990
America was in the midst
of a craze of spiritualism.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.358
During this time, the
practice of holding seances

00:04:34.358 --> 00:04:36.208
and seeking guidance through mediums

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:38.820
became increasingly popular
in the United States.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.070
So when St. Louis medium
Emily Grand Hutchings

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.267
claimed that her new book,
"Jap Herron" subtitled

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.030
"A Novel Written From the Ouija Board,"

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.190
was actually authored
by Mark Twain's ghost

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.106
as revealed to her by the Ouija board,

00:04:49.106 --> 00:04:52.800
many Americans bought the book
believing Hutchings' claims.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.686
Among those skeptical of
the book's ghostly origins,

00:04:54.686 --> 00:04:57.600
Mark Twain's Estate and Twain's publisher

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.860
who were so offended by the claims

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.020
they went straight to court
seeking to block publication

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.094
and have every copy of the book destroyed.

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:04.965
It all started in September, 1917

00:05:04.965 --> 00:05:06.780
when the book got national attention

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.480
following a scathing review
from the New York Times.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.501
Panning the book as quote,
"a feeble attempted imitation

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:13.284
of Twain's humor," the reviewer cast out

00:05:13.284 --> 00:05:15.640
on its ghostly origins by
noting the recent trend

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.940
of dead authors allegedly writing books

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:18.810
through Ouija boards.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.660
Quote, "if this is the best
that 'Mark Twain' can do

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:22.689
by reaching across the barrier,

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:25.394
the army of admirers that
his works have won for him

00:05:25.394 --> 00:05:29.460
will all hope that he will
hereafter respect that boundary."

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:31.410
In June, 1918, Mark Twain's estate

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:32.880
through his daughter, Clara Clemons,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.645
and Twain's publisher,
Harper and Brothers,

00:05:34.645 --> 00:05:35.851
went to the US Supreme Court

00:05:35.851 --> 00:05:38.040
to file an extra large copyright suit

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.890
against the medium and her
publisher, Mitchell Kennerley.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.570
The complete alleged Harper and Brothers

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.666
held the exclusive right to
Twain's stories for 17 years.

00:05:44.666 --> 00:05:47.400
And Hutchings and Mitchell Kennerley

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.380
were infringing on that right.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.120
The complaint asserts
that quote "'Jap Herron'

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.460
is far below the grade of
anything Mark Twain wrote

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.770
while alive and the
circulation of the book

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.150
would harm his reputation."

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.830
The complaint further
alleged that Hutchings

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.435
approached Harpers with a book in 1916,

00:06:00.435 --> 00:06:03.457
but Twain's publisher refused
to publish it on quote,

00:06:03.457 --> 00:06:05.320
"the ground that it didn't
emanate from Mark Twain

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:07.516
and had no literary merit."

00:06:07.516 --> 00:06:09.600
For relief, plaintiffs requested

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.670
that the Supreme Court blocked
publication of the book,

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:12.840
have all copies destroyed,

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.960
and require full accounting of
all illegally earned profits.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.360
However, the plaintiff's
victory was by no means assured.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.010
To establish copyright infringement,

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.249
they'd have to prove
definitively that a ghost

00:06:21.249 --> 00:06:24.900
spoke through a Ouija board,
which was no easy task.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.239
And as the New York Times
reported at the time,

00:06:26.239 --> 00:06:28.192
quote, "there is of
course ground for doubt

00:06:28.192 --> 00:06:30.262
whether testimony transmitted
through a Ouija board

00:06:30.262 --> 00:06:31.673
will be accepted.

00:06:31.673 --> 00:06:32.970
The court may consider it

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.534
incompetent, irrelevant, and immaterial."

00:06:34.534 --> 00:06:37.503
And as kind of amazing the
kind of hedging of bets

00:06:37.503 --> 00:06:39.930
that the New York Times was
doing with the word 'may'

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.186
in that particular sentence.

00:06:41.186 --> 00:06:43.860
But anyway, the plaintiffs here put forth

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.680
a clever two-step legal strategy
to corner the defendants.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:47.513
On the one hand, if Twain's ghost

00:06:47.513 --> 00:06:48.907
did actually write the book,

00:06:48.907 --> 00:06:50.782
the defendants have committed
copyright infringement

00:06:50.782 --> 00:06:52.410
and must return all profits

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.360
made to Twain's estate and publisher.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.520
On the other hand, the mediums
could avoid a copyright claim

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:57.645
by simply admitting that Twain's ghost

00:06:57.645 --> 00:06:59.580
didn't actually write the book,

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.260
which would expose them as liars.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.209
So stuck between the rock of
massive copyright exposure

00:07:03.209 --> 00:07:05.331
and the hard place of
admitting to be frauds,

00:07:05.331 --> 00:07:07.650
the mediums agreed to halt
publication of the book

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:08.961
and remove all copies from stores.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:11.799
The copyright trial of the
century would never come to pass,

00:07:11.799 --> 00:07:13.560
but Hutchings would continue to insist

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.828
that she was telling the
truth until her death in 1960.

00:07:15.828 --> 00:07:18.152
Though Mark Twain famously
quipped, "if you tell the truth

00:07:18.152 --> 00:07:19.980
you don't have to remember anything."

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.687
So whether Hutchings was
a liar with a great memory

00:07:22.687 --> 00:07:26.808
or an unjustly mocked prophet,
I suppose we'll never know.

00:07:26.808 --> 00:07:29.520
(girl screams)
Ooh!

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:30.820
But that takes us to a
couple of ghostly cases

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:32.270
coming out of Texas.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.258
They say that everything's
bigger in Texas,

00:07:34.258 --> 00:07:36.253
but you know what else is bigger in Texas?

00:07:36.253 --> 00:07:38.910
Cases where people took
financial advice from ghosts

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.460
only to claim fraud when
the advice didn't pan out.

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:42.763
You know, that old chestnut.

00:07:42.763 --> 00:07:46.092
Because in the early 1900s,
Texas courts addressed the issue

00:07:46.092 --> 00:07:47.664
of ghostly misrepresentation

00:07:47.664 --> 00:07:49.890
in both civil and criminal contexts.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.840
In the case of Burchill versus Hermsmeyer,

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.262
Bell Burchill claimed ghosts told her

00:07:53.262 --> 00:07:56.040
that there was substantial
oil under her land.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.970
- I drink your milkshake!

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.220
- Believing this to be true, HC Hermsmeyer

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.836
invested $10,000, about
$175,000 in today's dollars,

00:08:05.836 --> 00:08:08.010
into Burchill's oil company.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:09.991
When no oil was found, Hermsmeyer sued

00:08:09.991 --> 00:08:11.490
to recover his investment,

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.538
arguing that Burch's claim

00:08:12.538 --> 00:08:15.600
constituted a fraudulent
misrepresentation of fact.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.250
But the Texas Court of Appeals disagreed,

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.050
ruling that ghosts cannot form the basis

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.202
of a fraudulent misrepresentation,

00:08:20.202 --> 00:08:22.775
because the existence or
non-existence of ghosts

00:08:22.775 --> 00:08:25.980
is uncertain and a matter
of belief, not fact.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.690
And a similar result
occurred in the criminal case

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:29.010
of Nurse versus State.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.767
Jim Nurse, a self-described spiritualist,

00:08:30.767 --> 00:08:32.820
claimed that he could speak to ghosts

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.144
who told him a large amount of money

00:08:34.144 --> 00:08:37.514
was buried under the property
of one Mr. Alexander.

00:08:37.514 --> 00:08:38.843
- I've heavily invested in gold,

00:08:38.843 --> 00:08:41.037
which I've buried in
several different locations

00:08:41.037 --> 00:08:42.633
around Pawnee.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.060
Or have I?

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.620
- After someone working
on Mr. Alexander's home

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.210
found money buried by
the house's foundation,

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:52.329
nurse advised Alexander to
rebury the money immediately,

00:08:52.329 --> 00:08:55.694
because otherwise the
spirits would vanish it all.

00:08:55.694 --> 00:08:58.765
Nurse allegedly returned
later to dig up the money.

00:08:58.765 --> 00:09:01.530
Nurse was swiftly arrested
and convicted of the crime

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:04.350
of swindling, the early
20th century term for fraud.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.030
Now, the state argued
nurse was a charlatan

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.430
who fraudulently induced
Alexander to part with his money

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.740
by falsely claiming he
could talk to ghosts.

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.240
But like Burchill, the court of appeals

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.800
threw out the conviction on the grounds

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.020
that whether or not
Nurse can talk to ghosts

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:18.090
is an unknowable fact that cannot serve

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.500
as the basis for a fraud claim.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.930
Quote, "there is no evidence
in regard to the matter

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.302
one way or the other that
is as to whether or not

00:09:24.302 --> 00:09:26.053
appellant conversed with the spirits

00:09:26.053 --> 00:09:28.950
or called them up and
tested their veracity."

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:30.060
Ultimately, the court concluded

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.190
that Alexander was neither
deceived nor misled

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:33.626
because the alleged fraudulent claims

00:09:33.626 --> 00:09:35.810
that money was buried
under Alexander's house

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.620
was actually true.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.066
And since the underlying claim was true,

00:09:39.066 --> 00:09:42.240
whether Nurse could talk to
ghosts was legally irrelevant

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.880
and Nurse's swindling
conviction was reversed.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:46.411
But that takes us to the
house that was haunted

00:09:46.411 --> 00:09:47.854
as a matter of law.

00:09:47.854 --> 00:09:50.296
Now, one of my favorite
Halloween traditions growing up

00:09:50.296 --> 00:09:53.220
was watching "The Simpsons"
Treehouse of Horror episodes.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:55.327
I still remember the very
first Treehouse segment,

00:09:55.327 --> 00:09:57.000
"Bad Dreamhouse," where
the Simpsons family

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.658
is horrified to discover the
mansion that they just bought

00:09:58.658 --> 00:10:01.680
is haunted and actively
trying to murder them.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.170
Now, obviously, this
is just a silly cartoon

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.040
and no one would ever
try to actually get out

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.410
of a real estate purchase

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:08.610
because the seller didn't mention

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:09.870
that the house was haunted, right?

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:12.830
Well, this actually happened
and it was successful.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:15.180
And this opinion, which is
taught in every law school

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:17.026
in the country, wasn't handed
down a hundred years ago

00:10:17.026 --> 00:10:20.379
during America's spiritualism
craze, but in 1991,

00:10:20.379 --> 00:10:23.610
less than one year after
the Simpsons episode aired.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:25.522
Yet another example of the
Simpsons predicting the future.

00:10:25.522 --> 00:10:27.704
- As you know, we've inherited
quite a budget crunch

00:10:27.704 --> 00:10:28.888
from President Trump.

00:10:28.888 --> 00:10:30.119
- So here's the story of America's

00:10:30.119 --> 00:10:31.690
first legally haunted house.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:34.380
Jeffrey and Patricia Stanbovsky
were a New York City couple

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:35.760
who decided to leave behind the big city

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.370
and move slightly upstate to
the village of Nyack, New York.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.110
While looking for a new
home, the Stanbovskys

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:42.450
fell in love with an
eight bedroom, 4.5 bath

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.789
Victorian mansion occupied
by George and Helen Ackley

00:10:44.789 --> 00:10:46.195
and their four kids.

00:10:46.195 --> 00:10:49.093
Mr. Stanbovsky agreed to buy
the property for $650,000

00:10:49.093 --> 00:10:52.140
with a $32,500 down payment.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.340
So what's the problem here?

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:54.990
Well, the sellers neglected to mention

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.055
that the house had a
decades long reputation

00:10:57.055 --> 00:10:58.363
for being haunted.

00:10:58.363 --> 00:11:01.020
A reputation that the Ackleys
themselves helped cultivate

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.330
by talking it up in the
local and national press.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.047
In the 1977 Readers Digest article,

00:11:05.047 --> 00:11:06.824
"Our Haunted House on the Hudson,"

00:11:06.824 --> 00:11:09.099
the Ackleys recounted the
family's many ghost interactions

00:11:09.099 --> 00:11:10.170
in the home.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.700
Most notably husband George claims

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:13.613
he saw a pair of disembodied
moccasin clad feet

00:11:13.613 --> 00:11:16.200
walk up the stairs while Helen claims

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.910
that she saw a man in a powdered wig

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:19.620
and Revolutionary War attire.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.457
The house was even a feature
on a walking tour of Nyack

00:11:21.457 --> 00:11:24.510
and periodically attracted
paranormal enthusiasts.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:25.410
Now, after learning about

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.420
this previously undisclosed
ghostly history,

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.550
Mr. Stanbovsky filed
suit seeking rescission

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:30.840
of the real estate contract.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.602
But the trial court
dismissed Stanbovsky claims,

00:11:32.602 --> 00:11:33.812
citing caveat emptor.

00:11:33.812 --> 00:11:36.048
Caveat emptor, Latin for
let the buyer beware,

00:11:36.048 --> 00:11:38.580
imposes a duty on a buyer
to reasonably examine

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.610
the fitness and value of
property before purchase.

00:11:41.610 --> 00:11:43.680
And under this principle,
a buyer is barred

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:45.870
from rescinding a contract
on the basis of defects

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:47.070
that would've been discovered

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.810
through a reasonable investigation.

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:50.520
But Stanbovsky appealed this decision

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.200
and the court of appeals
reversed the trial court,

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.060
reasoning that because
the sellers themselves

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.652
were the source of the
home's haunted reputation

00:11:55.652 --> 00:11:58.500
and this fact that
materially impaired the value

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:00.270
of the property could
not easily be discovered

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:03.120
by even a diligent buyer,
the failure to disclose

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:04.620
was grounds to cancel the agreement.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.750
Quote, "whether the source
of the spectral apparition

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.569
seen by defendant seller are
parapsychic or psychogenic,

00:12:09.569 --> 00:12:11.102
having reported their presence

00:12:11.102 --> 00:12:13.011
in both a national publication,

00:12:13.011 --> 00:12:14.589
(Reader's Digest) and the local press

00:12:14.589 --> 00:12:16.695
(in 1977 and 1982, respectively),

00:12:16.695 --> 00:12:19.435
defendant is stopped to
deny their existence,

00:12:19.435 --> 00:12:20.800
and as a matter of law,

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:23.324
the house is haunted."
(spooky music)

00:12:23.324 --> 00:12:24.750
So what happened next?

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:27.166
Well, the Stanbovskys were
able to back out of the deal,

00:12:27.166 --> 00:12:29.114
but don't worry, the
Ackleys had no problem

00:12:29.114 --> 00:12:30.480
reselling the house.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.348
Since then, the house has
changed hands multiple times

00:12:32.348 --> 00:12:36.510
being most recently sold in
March, 2021 for $1.8 million.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.520
And in 2002, New York
replaced caveat emptor

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:39.575
with an affirmative duty to disclose.

00:12:39.575 --> 00:12:41.580
Sellers are now required to fill out

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:42.679
a standard form disclosure statement,

00:12:42.679 --> 00:12:45.360
containing 48 affirmative representations

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:46.462
about the condition of the property.

00:12:46.462 --> 00:12:50.100
Though sadly, zero
disclosures about ghosts.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.600
And there haven't been any hauntings

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.040
since the accolades left,

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.122
which some paranormal investigators

00:12:54.122 --> 00:12:57.060
attributed to the ghost being
bored of the new owners.

